k a certain way - Harsh self-criticism of his appearance If those aren’t present, it may simply be that he’s discovered a constructive outlet for creativity and self-care — something that actually *builds* real confidence, not masks insecurity. And even if there is some insecurity, the solution isn’t to ban the grooming; it’s to address the insecurity at its source through connection, support, and possibly professional help if needed. ### 3. Why “treating it differently for boys” doesn’t mean we should shame them The article you mention seems to suggest that because the dynamic *may* be different, adults sometimes react more harshly to boys who groom — perhaps interpreting it as a sign of fragility or overcompensation that doesn’t fit masculine norms. But I’d gently argue that the **harsh reaction itself can make the insecurity worse**. If a boy already feels insecure and then hears that his attempt to feel better (his grooming) is “wrong” for his gender, he learns to be ashamed of both his insecurity *and* his way of coping with it. That’s a recipe for deeper problems. Instead, we can acknowledge the potential gender difference (boys may indeed struggle silently more) and respond with greater sensitivity, not greater disapproval. ### 4. A constructive way to approach your son, informed by this insight With this psychologist’s perspective in mind, you might open a gentle, non-judgmental dialogue: > “Hey, I’ve noticed you’ve been really into your style lately — it looks like you enjoy it. I was reading something interesting that sometimes when guys get into grooming a lot, it can be about wanting to feel more confident or in control. No judgment at all — I’m just curious: for you, is it mostly fun and creative, or does it feel like pressure to look a certain way?” This gives him an invitation to reflect and share. It doesn’t assume there’s a problem, but it lets him know you’re a safe person to talk to if there is one. ### 5. Address the husband’s fear with this new information Your husband might have been partly right in sensing that this behavior *could* signal something different for a boy — and he might actually feel vindicated. That’s okay. You can say: > “You know, you were picking up on something real. Even the experts say that for some boys, this can be an insecurity thing. I think we both just want to make sure our son is truly okay. So what if we stop judging *what* he’s doing and instead focus on *how he’s doing*? If he’s happy, has friends, and feels good about himself apart from his looks, then the grooming is just his thing. If we see signs that he’s not okay, then we deal with that together — with love, not by telling him he’s wrong for caring.” This shifts the team from “police the action” to “monitor the well-being,” which is a much stronger and more supportive parental stance. ### 6. Remember that times have changed, and so have male role models It’s worth adding that while the teasing you recall was real in your experience, male grooming is far more normalized now. Many admired men — athletes, actors, musicians — spend considerable time and effort on their appearance. Your son isn’t necessarily going to be an outlier in his generation in the way a boy might have been twenty or thirty years ago. Peer norms shift, and he may already be navigating a world where his grooming is respected, not ridiculed. --- Ultimately, that article’s point doesn’t invalidate the idea that your son deserves respect and understanding. It actually reinforces the need for deeper, more compassionate attention — exactly the opposite of saying “this is wrong for a boy.” Would you like to talk through what specific signs of insecurity would make you concerned, so you can feel more confident in distinguishing a phase from a real problem?
